# Single source of truth for settings (R3)

The AIC PIM workspace has historically grown **two parallel stores**
for several binaries' settings:

- The product-specific JSON file (`%PROGRAMDATA%\AIC\OfflinePimAgent\settings.json`,
 `/etc/aic/pim-offline-agent/settings.json`) — used by the legacy
 import path.
- The `pim-app-config` platform store (Windows Registry under
 `HKLM\Software\AnalogInfo\<program_id>`, libsecret / Keychain +
 `/etc/aic/<program_id>/settings.toml` elsewhere) — used by the
 configurator GUIs and the modern manifest-driven flow.

When a binary reads both, the daemon's "effective" configuration
becomes a function of read order, file mtime, and whether the
configurator was run last. The post-2026-05-19 win32 configurator
audit caught a case where the agent read its old JSON file AFTER
the operator had updated the same key in HKLM via the configurator,
silently reverting the change. This rule prevents that class of bug
from recurring.

## The rule

> **Every binary in this workspace names exactly ONE canonical
> settings store in its `app-config.toml` manifest. Reads at
> runtime resolve through that store and only that store. Any
> second store that historically held the same data is a
> migration source — read once on startup if the canonical
> store has not been initialized for this machine, then written
> through to the canonical store and never read again. Writes
> ALWAYS go to the canonical store.**

Concretely:

1. **Every `app-config.toml` declares `canonical_store = "<kind>"`**
 (or the equivalent typed field once `pim-app-config` 0.x grows
 it). Today the canonical store is implicit in the
 `pim-config-storage` backend selection per OS (registry on
 Windows, libsecret + TOML elsewhere). When the typed field
 ships, the manifest must name it explicitly.
2. **The startup migration is one-shot.** On first daemon boot
 after upgrade, if the canonical store has no rows for this
 machine AND the legacy store has rows, the daemon copies each
 legacy value through `AppConfig::set_value` (so manifest
 validation runs — see R2), then renames the legacy file to
 `<name>.migrated-<ISO-8601-UTC>.json` (Linux/macOS) or sets a
 `Migrated` `REG_DWORD = 1` sentinel under the legacy key
 (Windows). Subsequent boots see the sentinel and skip the
 migration entirely.
3. **The migration runs through the manifest gate** (R2). If a
 legacy file contains a key the new manifest does not declare,
 the daemon logs a `warn!` with the orphan list (no values) and
 leaves the orphan in the legacy file for the operator. It
 does NOT silently drop the orphan and it does NOT write it to
 the canonical store.
4. **Writes ALWAYS go to the canonical store.** No code path may
 write the legacy file after the migration. The configurator
 GUIs writing through `AppConfig::set_value` already satisfy
 this; legacy `Settings::save_to_file` paths must be removed or
 renamed to `Settings::save_to_legacy_file_for_export_only` so
 the intent is unambiguous.

## Per-binary canonical-store table

This table is the single source of truth for "where does this
binary read its settings from at runtime". Update it in the same
change set when a binary's store changes. The detailed per-platform
paths live in
[`pim-app-config/docs/CONFIGURATION_STORAGE.md`](../../pim-app-config/docs/CONFIGURATION_STORAGE.md);
this table summarizes the canonical / legacy split.

| Binary | Canonical store | Legacy migration source (one-shot) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pim-offline-server` | `pim-app-config` platform store, program id `pim-offline-server` (HKLM on Windows, libsecret + `/etc/aic/pim-offline-server/settings.toml` elsewhere) | none — greenfield |
| `pim-offline-agent` | `pim-app-config` platform store, program id `pim-offline-agent` (HKLM on Windows, libsecret + `/etc/aic/pim-offline-agent/settings.toml` elsewhere) | server-produced `settings.json` imported via `pim-offline-agent import-settings`; **NOT** read at runtime — see TODO below |
| `pim-offline-client-configurator-{win32,tauri}` | same as `pim-offline-agent` (the configurator edits the agent's store) | n/a |
| `pim-offline-client-elevate-{win32,tauri}` | none (writes nothing; reads pipe path only) | n/a |
| `pim-offline-server-configurator-{win32,tauri}` | same as `pim-offline-server` (the configurator edits the server's store) | n/a |
| `pim-app-config` (sample CLI) | the manifest passed via `--manifest` | n/a |

